WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:07.470
This week Council held its monthly meeting
for May.

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:11.840
After receiving a verbal submission on the
Long Term Plan from local resident, Mr. Robert

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:14.719
Johnston, the council moved into receiving
reports.

00:00:14.719 --> 00:00:20.230
First up, was a regeneration report on proposed
dredging in the Kaiapoi River.

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:25.530
The Council authorised staff to proceed with
design, consenting and work programming of

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:31.310
dredging that will increase navigability along
the Kaiapoi River, particularly at low tide.

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:35.470
Dredging is also planned for the north side
of the river near the William Street Bridge.

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:39.620
This will allow for the installation of floating
pontoons alongside the Riverview terraces

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:42.080
that are currently under construction.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:45.900
Council approved the design and layout of
the pontoons and staff will now put the purchase

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:48.180
of the pontoons themselves out to tender.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:55.040
The Council also expressed its support for
Environment Canterbury’s plans to fill gaps

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:58.340
in the secondary stop bank along the Ashley
River.

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:03.530
As part of this work, ECAN will be raising
both Cones Road &amp; Milton Road approximately

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:07.460
3 metres and that work is expected to take place
later this year.

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:12.360
Although traffic diversions will be in place
during the road works, access to nearby facilities

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:16.920
such as the Dog Park and BMX track will be
maintained, as well as access to the Ashley

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:18.050
Bridge itself.

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:21.780
The project will be fully funded by Environment
Canterbury.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:28.630
Following community consultation, the Waimakariri
District Council Stormwater Drainage and Watercourse

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:32.030
Protection Bylaw 2018 was adopted.

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:36.940
The bylaw has a range of provisions that aim
to protect our storm water and drainage infrastructure,

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:41.610
mitigate flooding hazards and safeguard the
environment against the discharging of contaminants.

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:46.840
It also makes clearer to landowners their
responsibilities around drains and waterways.

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:54.020
The Council also approved a draft submission
to the upcoming Government Policy Statement

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:55.500
on Land Transport.

00:01:56.010 --> 00:02:00.280
This submission is seen as an important opportunity
for the Council to present its views about

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:04.740
the new Government’s proposals for priorities
and investment in land transport.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:09.080
In our submission we expressed our support
for more public transport options for the

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:13.940
Greater Christchurch area and we strongly
advocated for the continuation of the proposed

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:16.120
NZTA Woodend Bypass.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:22.720
The next monthly Council meeting will be at
the Council Chambers in Rangiora on Tuesday

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:24.840
5th of June at 1pm.

